{
  "_disclaimer": "SIMULATED DEMONSTRATION CONFIGURATION. Escalation triggers for a prototype. Not a validated screening instrument. The system does not claim to detect any condition; it identifies language and values that MAY indicate a life-threatening situation and escalates them for human attention.",
  "_what_went_wrong_before": "The first version had ten categories, all of them medical: cardiac, respiratory, neurological, allergic, overdose. A patient said 'I have been in a fatal car accident, my friend is dead and my leg is amputated' and the system scored zero and called it NORMAL. Not a gap in the list -- a gap in the WAY the list was built. Enumerating the emergencies somebody thought of at the time is not a safety net, it is a record of that afternoon's imagination.",
  "_how_it_is_built_now": "Three layers, and the phrase list is now the smallest of them. (1) The MODEL layer reads what the patient said and judges whether it may be life-threatening, with no list involved -- this is the primary layer whenever a key is configured. (2) The MECHANISM layer matches catastrophic events and body-part catastrophes by category rather than by symptom, so 'amputated', 'crushed', 'run over', 'stabbed' and 'not breathing' all reach the gate regardless of which body system they belong to. (3) The RULE layer below is a floor for the phrases most likely to arrive verbatim. Any layer can fire alone. None can silence another.",
